# The five cells of the validation battery.
#
# Three cells reproduce an application bucket: the bucket's CPU per request and
# the median span count measured for applications in that bucket. Their measured
# overhead is compared against the range published for that bucket.
#
# Two cells carry a deliberately high span count. Their job is to measure the
# per-span cost where it is large enough to see. The second repeats the same
# span count at four times the CPU per request, to show the per-span cost does
# not depend on it.
#
#   spans = 1 server + JDBC + REDIS + 2 x HTTP   (in this application Odigos
#   emits two client spans per outbound HTTP call: the HTTP client library's,
#   and the connection's underneath it)
#
# RATE holds the uninstrumented pod at 0.6-0.95 of a core (under half of its
# 2 vCPUs), clear of the point where both hyperthreads saturate.
#
# UNITS are the values calibrated on the reference nodes (AWS m6i.2xlarge, Intel
# Xeon 3rd gen). They are NOT portable: on any other machine type, run the
# calibration step in VALIDATION.md and apply the units it prints, so each cell
# lands on its TARGET_MS. RATE stays as it is once TARGET_MS is met.
#
#   s        bucket "under 2 ms"    published range 2.6 - 17.2%
#   schatty  bucket "2 to 4 ms"     published range 1.3 - 9.2%
#   m        bucket "4 to 8 ms"     published range 0.9 - 3.9%
#   l        per-span cost, 20 spans on a 3.5 ms request
#   xl       per-span cost, 20 spans on a 14 ms request
#
# NAME     PRESET  RATE  K6_CPU  UNITS  TARGET_MS  SPANS  JDBC  REDIS  HTTP  DELAY_MS
s          S        500  2.0      31    1.5        1      0     0      0     10
schatty    S        300  1.2      68    3.0        2      1     0      0     10
m          S        150  0.8     146    6.0        2      1     0      0     10
l          S        199  1.4      46    3.5        20     8     5      3     10
xl         M         58  0.5     296    14         20     8     5      3     10
